{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:FluidDynamics.CoreLeviForm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"FluidDynamics.CoreLeviForm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.CrossProduct"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.core_levi_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.core_levi_form","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L107-L125","doc":"**Core Levi form** ([`prop:core-levi-form`], re-scoped 2026-10-13).\n\nFor `Θ = f·ξ♭` with unit `ξ`, `C = curl ξ`, `g = ∇f` at a point:\n\n* the restriction of `dΘ = df ∧ ξ♭ + f·dξ♭` to `ker Θ = ξ^⊥` is\n  `f·(ξ·C)` times the area form — on `ξ^⊥` the `df ∧ ξ♭` term vanishes and\n  `dξ♭(v, w) = C·(v × w)`; and\n* the vorticity helicity density is `(f ξ)·(f C + g × ξ) = f²·(ξ·C)`.\n\nBoth conjuncts are derived from `levi_restriction_rank_one` and\n`helicity_rescale`; neither is a hypothesis restated. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:core-levi-form","label":"prop:core-levi-form","md":"quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.levi_restriction_rank_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.levi_restriction_rank_one","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L94-L105","doc":"**The Levi form on `ker Θ` has rank one.**  For a unit `ξ` and `v, w ∈ ξ^⊥`,\n`C·(v × w) = (ξ·C)·(ξ·(v × w))`.  Read with `C = curl ξ`: `dξ♭` restricted to\nthe plane `ξ^⊥` is the scalar `ξ·curl ξ` times the plane's area form\n`ξ·(v × w)`.  So the Levi datum of `ξ^⊥` is one number, not an eigenvalue\npair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.levi_restriction_key","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.levi_restriction_key","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L83-L92","doc":"Binet–Cauchy rearranged, with no hypotheses:\n`(ξ·ξ)(C·(v×w)) − (ξ·C)(ξ·(v×w)) = ((ξ×C)·v)(ξ·w) − ((ξ×C)·w)(ξ·v)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.contact_iff_rescale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.contact_iff_rescale","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L72-L81","doc":"**The contact criterion does not depend on the scale `f`.**  Over a ring\nwithout zero divisors, for `f ≠ 0`: `f²·s ≠ 0 ↔ s ≠ 0`.  With\n`s = ξ·curl ξ` and `Θ ∧ dΘ = f²·s·vol` this says `ker(f ξ♭)` is contact exactly\nwhere `ξ·curl ξ ≠ 0`; with `helicity_rescale` (and `f = |ω|`) it is the same\nlocus as `ω·curl ω ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.helicity_rescale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.helicity_rescale","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L62-L70","doc":"**Identity D2.**  With `ω = f ξ` and `curl ω = f·curl ξ + ∇f × ξ` (product\nrule; `C = curl ξ`, `g = ∇f` at the point), the vorticity helicity density is\n`ω·curl ω = f²·(ξ·curl ξ)`.  The `∇f` term drops because `ξ·(g × ξ) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.leviScalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.CoreLevi.leviScalar","kind":"def","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/core-levi-form.lean#L58-L60","doc":"**The Levi scalar** of the plane field `ξ^⊥` at a point: `ξ·curl ξ`, given\nthe values `ξ` and `C = curl ξ` there. ","signatureChunk":0}]}